{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LCA36yLZbb33hmcJottgMG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me write the paper. I'll first copy the acl.sty to the output directory, then write the paper.tex file.\n\nLet me organize the paper carefully:\n\n1. Abstract (~200 words)\n2. Introduction (1.5 pages)\n3. Related Work (0.75 page)\n4. Method / Analytical Framework (1 page)\n5. Experiments (3.5 pages)\n   - 5.1 Task-Dependent Convergence\n   - 5.2 Architecture Expansion\n   - 5.3 Per-Example Divergence\n   - 5.4 Sample Characterization\n   - 5.5 Attention Pattern Analysis\n   - 5.6 Scale-Dependent Convergence\n   - 5.7 Convergence-Divergence Co-evolution\n6. Discussion (0.5 page)\n7. Limitations (0.25 page)\n8. Conclusion (0.25 page)\n\nThat's about 8 pages total. I need to remember the style constraints: no em-dashes or en-dashes except in number ranges, and avoid -ing endings. Let me start writing now.\n\nI'm being careful about several key things: pulling all the experiment data from the JSON file, following the framing guidance, not overclaiming results, properly engaging with related work, reporting statistics honestly, making sure I differentiate from Wang et al., and keeping the narrative guidance out of the actual paper. I'll draft the paper mentally first, starting with the title \"Task-Dependent Convergence and Per-Example Divergence in Hybrid Recurrent Architectures\" or something similar, then work through each section systematically. Now I'm mapping out the experimental structure and realizing I've planned way too many tables for the page limit—twelve main tables plus three supplementary ones won't fit in an eight-page paper, so I need to consolidate significantly.\n\nLooking at what's truly essential: the cross-architecture convergence data needs to be front and center, combined with the new benchmark results. The per-example Jaccard scores and effect magnitudes are critical for showing the mechanism. The helped/hurt characterization can be condensed into a single table rather than split across experiments. The attention patterns and co-evolution dynamics are the signature findings, so those stay. The scale comparison showing the 340M rebound effect deserves inclusion, and I should keep the statistical rigor with BH-FDR correction summary. I'm narrowing this down to roughly six to eight core tables that tell the complete story without overwhelming the main text. I'm realizing I need to be more selective about what goes in the main text versus supplementary materials. The Wang et al. comparison can move to the appendix, and I can consolidate some tables—merging the per-example divergence with co-evolution data, and keeping the attention pattern details as a supplementary table instead. This gets me down to 6 core tables for the main paper while preserving all the key results.\n\nNow I'm shifting focus to actually writing the LaTeX. I need to be strict about formatting: avoiding em-dashes, using booktabs for clean tables, writing self-contained captions, and pulling all numbers directly from the experiment data. I'm also reconsidering the overall structure—combining related experiments into shared sections will help with space efficiency while keeping the paper under 8 pages and still covering all the work comprehensively. Looking at the benchmark analysis across different task types and architectures, I'm examining convergence patterns through retrieval-demand taxonomy and confirmatory tests on specific benchmarks, then expanding to include Mamba2 and applying multiple comparison corrections. For the per-example analysis, I'm measuring divergence through Jaccard similarity and effect magnitudes across samples, characterizing which examples show within-architecture consistency versus cross-architecture disagreement, and tracing how attention patterns correlate with these divergence patterns.\n\nNow I'm moving into scale effects—comparing how the 1.3B model shows monotonic convergence while the 340M model exhibits a rebound pattern, which suggests a capacity bottleneck mechanism at play. The discussion will connect these findings to prior work and draw out the implications, then address limitations before concluding. I'm verifying the exact numerical values from the data to ensure precision in the LaTeX writeup. Looking at the Jaccard similarity continuing to drop for HellaSwag while the retrieval-demand correlation is strongly negative at -0.92. For the helped/hurt analysis, the within-architecture metrics show no significant effects, but the cross-architecture Kruskal-Wallis test reveals a significant hurt effect. The attention correlations across all three architectures are moderate and significant, clustering around 0.36 on average.\n\nNow examining the cross-architecture cosine similarity at 0.21 and the attention focus windows, which vary by architecture—GDN concentrates on earlier tokens while RetNet and HGRN2 spread across wider ranges. The architecture expansion experiment shows a strong negative correlation across four architectures with an 80% reduction in standard deviation, and Mamba2 exhibits similar patterns though with weaker significance. The 340M model shows a rebound effect beginning at the h12-1 layer.\n\nLooking at the rebound samples, 145 instances cluster heavily in the top difficulty quartile, with the 340M model struggling at 4.62 perplexity compared to 1.3B's 3.15. The statistical robustness checks reveal that after multiple comparison corrections, only two tests remain significant under BH-FDR with m=5, while Bonferroni correction leaves just one borderline result, and the full set of nine comparisons yields no significant findings.\n\nNow examining the co-evolution patterns across model checkpoints—standard deviation decreases while effect magnitude grows, and the Jaccard similarity steadily declines, with a strong negative correlation of -1.0 between these trajectories. Testing this pattern on additional benchmarks shows StoryCloze replicates the strong effect with 79% variance reduction, but BoolQ and COPA show progressively weaker relationships, suggesting the phenomenon isn't universal across all language understanding tasks.\n\nTime to draft the paper itself, being mindful of ACL formatting conventions. I'll use the thebibliography environment directly to keep things self-contained without needing separate bibtex compilation. For the tables, I need to apply booktabs styling with top, middle, and bottom rules instead of horizontal lines and no vertical dividers. When defining the attention ratio notation, I should clarify that this follows Wang et al.'s convention where h_a represents attention layers and h_r represents recurrent layers.\n\nLooking at the experimental trajectory across the standard deviation measurements, I can see the progression: h0 is purely recurrent with no attention, then h24-1 introduces minimal attention with one layer per 24 recurrent layers, h12-1 doubles that frequency, h6-1 increases it further, and h3-1 maximizes attention with one layer per 3 recurrent layers. As the attention ratio increases through this sequence, the standard deviation drops significantly from 0.024 down to 0.005, which aligns with the hypothesis. I'll refer to this as the \"attention ratio\" in the paper.\n\nNow I'm ready to write this up. Let me gather the bibliography—I need citations for the model zoo work, underspecification theory, ensemble diversity metrics, dataset cartography, statistical correction methods, and the various architectural papers like RetNet, Mamba, and the gating mechanisms.\n\nI also need to pull in the benchmark papers and make sure I'm hitting the reference count requirement. The framing document is clear: at least 20 references, avoid \"mechanistic analysis\" language in favor of \"characterization\" or \"decomposition,\" and position this as a community analysis rather than a re-analysis. For the statistical approach, I'll lead with Benjamini-Hochberg FDR on the pre-specified retrieval subset, with Bonferroni mentioned as an alternative.\n\nI'm going to report the m=5 results honestly and acknowledge the full m=9 failure upfront. Now I need to write the actual paper while staying under 8 pages for the main text. One critical constraint I'm noticing: the style guide prohibits em-dashes and en-dashes except in number ranges and proper nouns, and I can't end sentences with -ing phrases. Paragraphs should run 5-8 sentences to avoid over-segmentation. Let me also copy the ACL style file to the output directory so compilation works smoothly.\n\nFor the tables, I need to be strategic about space. I'm planning four main tables: one consolidating all 9 benchmarks with convergence metrics across different configurations, another showing how four different architectures perform on LAMBADA specifically, a third breaking down per-example divergence patterns by benchmark, and a fourth for sample characterization. This layout should let me fit everything while staying within the page limit. I'm debating whether to worry about page length for this draft—quality matters more than fitting a strict page count, so I'll write comprehensively. For the supplementary materials, I'm planning five additional tables covering the full correction details, sensitivity analyses, and methodological comparisons. Now I need to structure the LaTeX using the ACL template and settle on a title that captures the core finding without sounding too formulaic—something like \"Task-Dependent Convergence and Per-Example Divergence Under Attention in Hybrid Recurrent Architectures\" feels more natural than the colon-heavy alternatives.\n\nLooking at the framing document's headline about how attention reduces cross-architecture variance by 79% on LAMBADA while affecting nearly non-overlapping sample subsets, I'm weighing a few title options that emphasize both the aggregate convergence and the per-example divergence. I'll go with something direct and informative that leads with the per-example analysis angle.\n\nNow I'm setting up the LaTeX document structure with the ACL template, defining notation conventions for correlation coefficients and p-values, and pulling the actual experimental numbers to write a thorough, complete paper.\n\nSetting up the document... I'm drafting the full LaTeX document now, thinking through each section carefully. Starting with the abstract—I need to summarize the key findings about how attention affects convergence across different hybrid architectures, noting the 79% variance reduction on retrieval tasks and the broader implications for model behavior. Hybrid architectures combining linear recurrence with attention layers are now standard practice, but we lack quantitative understanding of what attention actually does at the per-example level—Wang et al.'s extensive model zoo reveals qualitative convergence across architectures, yet practitioners still need to know whether similar aggregate performance masks meaningful per-example differences. This question of whether models with comparable overall metrics diverge in their individual predictions is well-established in other domains.\n\nI'm providing the first systematic quantification using Wang et al.'s model zoo plus additional Mamba2 variants, testing three core claims about task-dependent convergence, scale-dependent convergence, and per-example divergence through exact permutation tests. The analysis reveals that convergence strength correlates with task characteristics like retrieval demand, with particularly strong effects on benchmarks like LAMBADA and StoryCloze.\n\nNow I'm looking at the related work on hybrid recurrent architectures, covering the major models like RetNet, HGRN2, Mamba variants, and practical implementations like Jamba and RecurrentGemma.\n\nI'm also connecting this to the underspecification literature—how models with identical aggregate performance can diverge on individual predictions—and bringing in ensemble diversity measures like Jaccard similarity that I'll apply to these hybrid architectures.\n\nThen I'm grounding the per-example analysis in prior work on dataset cartography and data-centric NLP, which lets me combine fine-grained prediction divergence with cross-architecture comparisons.\n\nFor the model zoo, I'm working with a large set of hybrid recurrent models across different attention ratios and scales, including a Mamba2-attention variant, and evaluating them systematically using standard benchmarking tools.\n\nTo measure convergence, I'm tracking how standard deviation changes across attention ratios and testing whether that ordering is statistically significant using rank correlation and permutation tests.\n\nFinally, I'm decomposing predictions at the example level by computing similarity metrics between model pairs to see where and how they diverge.\n\nNow I'm setting up the statistical framework with multiple correction strategies depending on whether tests are exploratory or confirmatory, and organizing the 9 benchmarks into a task taxonomy based on their retrieval demands.\n\nLooking at the confirmatory results, StoryCloze shows the strongest effect with near-perfect negative correlation and substantial variance reduction, while BoolQ and COPA show weaker patterns. The multiple comparison corrections reveal how sensitive the findings are to the correction method—significant under BH-FDR but disappearing under stricter Bonferroni correction, which I'm reporting transparently.\n\nNow I'm moving through the per-example divergence analysis with detailed metrics across benchmarks, examining how individual samples behave within and across architectures, and looking at attention patterns to understand the mechanisms driving convergence. I'm also tracking how model behaviors co-evolve during training and comparing convergence patterns across different model scales.\n\nFor the discussion, I need to clearly differentiate this work from Wang et al.'s approach, spell out what practitioners should take away, and address the underspecification problem in hybrid architectures. I'm about to write the full LaTeX document, being careful with dash formatting throughout. I'm settling on using thebibliography to keep the compilation straightforward with just pdflatex. I'll define macros for repeated numerical values to prevent transcription errors, and I'm ready to draft the full paper now. The title will focus on per-example analysis of attention convergence in hybrid recurrent models, with an abstract around 175 words.\n\nNow I'm laying out the paper structure: an introduction establishing the gap in quantitative understanding of attention's per-example effects across four architectures, related work covering hybrid architectures and ensemble diversity, and an analytical framework section detailing the model setup, convergence metrics, and per-example decomposition approach.\n\nThe results section spans task-dependent convergence with benchmark summaries and trajectory tables, per-example divergence analysis examining Jaccard similarity and attention correlations, and scale-dependent convergence comparisons. I'm ready to start writing the actual LaTeX, beginning with the co-evolution table that combines trajectory data showing how attention patterns shift across architectures. I'm comparing the standard deviation trajectories across different architecture configurations—the 4-arch setup with Mamba2 shows higher initial variance than the 3-arch version, but both converge similarly as attention layers increase. Now I need to clarify the attention ratio notation in the paper, mapping each configuration to its actual percentage of attention layers, since this distinction is crucial for understanding the results. I'm settling on using Wang et al.'s notation directly and defining it clearly in the paper rather than converting to percentages. Now I need to actually start writing, keeping in mind this should target EMNLP Main-level quality for the 2026 submission.\n\nSince this is a review-ready draft rather than a polished submission, I can prioritize getting the content and structure right. I'll use compact table formatting with smaller fonts where needed, and rely on tables rather than figures to present the key analyses. Let me start building the LaTeX document now, being careful about formatting conventions like avoiding em-dashes. I'm identifying which values to bold in each table—the strongest convergence results in Table 1, the highest convergence row in Table 2, and the most divergent metric in Table 4. I'll need to include xcolor and multirow packages for formatting, then structure the paper across six main sections covering the analytical framework, task-dependent convergence, per-example divergence, and scale effects.\n\nFor the narrative flow, I'm reconsidering the claim order—starting with task-dependent convergence to establish the phenomenon, then per-example divergence to reveal the paradox, followed by the co-evolution bridge and scale analysis as boundary conditions, since the core insight really hinges on showing both aggregate convergence and per-example divergence together.\n\nI'm settling on a structure with task-dependent convergence covering cross-architecture patterns and the retrieval-demand taxonomy, per-example divergence examining architecture-specific samples and attention patterns, and scale-dependent convergence as the final section. I should also make sure the Mamba2 data fits into the task-dependent section since it expands the architecture coverage.\n\nNow I'm looking at the standard deviation trajectories across different experimental runs. The 4-architecture setup shows a consistent reduction from 0.029 down to 0.006, while the 3-architecture baseline achieves about 79% reduction. The co-evolution data from the later experiment traces a similar path, confirming that the pattern holds across different model configurations.\n\nFor the paper structure, I need to allocate space carefully across the sections. With roughly 4000-4500 words of actual text available in an 8-page ACL format, I'm planning to front-load the convergence analysis with the 3-architecture results in a main table, then use the 4-architecture expansion to validate the findings without duplicating content.\n\nNow I'm deciding on the bibliography approach. Since I want to avoid external dependencies, I'll use thebibliography with manual entries formatted for natbib compatibility rather than relying on a separate .bib file and bibtex compilation. Formatting bibliography entries...\n\nI need to use the natbib author-year format that ACL expects, where each entry follows the pattern of `\\bibitem[{ShortCite(Year)}]{key}`. This will let `\\citet` and `\\citep` work correctly without needing bibtex. Let me list out all the references I need to format: Wang et al. for the model zoo, D'Amour et al. on underspecification, Krogh & Vedelsby on ensemble diversity, Kuncheva & Whitaker on diversity measures, Swayamdipta et al. on dataset cartography, Benjamini & Hochberg on FDR correction, Sun et al. on RetNet, Qin et al. on HGRN2, and Gu & Dao on Mamba.\n\nContinuing with the remaining references: Dao & Gu for Mamba2, Yang et al. for GatedDeltaNet, Lieber et al. for Jamba, Botev et al. for RecurrentGemma, Peng et al. for RWKV, Vaswani et al. for the original Transformer, Paperno et al. for LAMBADA, Mostafazadeh et al. for StoryCloze, Zellers et al. for HellaSwag, Clark et al. for ARC, Sakaguchi et al. for Winogrande, Hendrycks et al. for MMLU, and Clark et al. for BLEU.\n\nThat brings me to 25 references, which satisfies the requirement. Now I'm ready to write the full paper, making sure to weave the experimental results into a coherent narrative organized by topic rather than listing them as numbered experiments. I need to remember that EMNLP 2026 requires a Limitations section, so I'll include that instead of leaving it empty. I should also verify the Wang et al. citation details—the arXiv ID is 2507.06457—before finalizing the references.\n\nI'm going to stop second-guessing myself and start writing. For the GatedDeltaNet reference, I'll cite it as Yang et al. (2024) based on the experiment data. I'll use thebibliography environment since we're working with pdflatex directly rather than bibtex. Now I'm structuring the tables carefully—Table 1 will show cross-architecture convergence across benchmarks using the 1.3B model variants.\n\nGenerating markdown table... Looking at the standard deviation trajectories for BoolQ and COPA, I can see the reduction percentages are 45% and 19% respectively. Since I'm missing standard deviation data for several benchmarks like HellaSwag, ARC-C, and Winogrande, I should organize the table to separate what I have complete data for—LAMBADA, StoryCloze, BoolQ, and COPA—from the benchmarks where I only have correlation values. I'm mapping out which benchmarks have complete data versus partial. LAMBADA and StoryCloze have the full picture with correlation, p-value, and standard deviation reduction. The original five benchmarks only give me correlation coefficients, while the three newer ones have varying levels of detail—BoolQ and COPA include std reduction but HellaSwag's p-value is missing entirely. I'm working through the p-value calculations for the benchmarks with lower correlations—for ρ values like 0.3, 0.6, and 0.1 with n=5, the p-values would be quite large (likely >0.2), so I'll mark those as non-significant rather than computing exact values. I'll use the specific p-values I have from the data and indicate the rest as n.s. to keep the table clean.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the actual LaTeX document. I need to set up the bibliography entries with the proper natbib format, and I'm also thinking about consolidating Tables 4 and 7 since the co-evolution analysis is really just examining how the per-example divergence changes over time.\n\nWriting the complete paper... I'm laying out the remaining tables: Table 3 shows LAMBADA standard deviations across 3 and 4 architectures, Table 4 presents per-example divergence metrics by benchmark, Table 5 covers co-evolution patterns, Table 6 characterizes sample distributions within and across architectures, Table 7 analyzes attention pattern correlations, and Table 8 begins a scale comparison analysis.\n\nLooking at this more carefully, eight tables is pushing it for space constraints. I should consolidate—Tables 2, 3, 7, and 8 can move into the main text as supporting details, while Tables 1, 4, 5, and 6 are the core findings that deserve dedicated space. Actually, let me expand that slightly to six main tables: the full benchmark results, the LAMBADA trajectory with Mamba2, per-example divergence, co-evolution metrics, sample characterization, and attention pattern analysis.\n\nI also need to account for the fact that attention patterns only have data for GDN, RetNet, and HGRN2 (not Mamba2), while the per-example analysis covers LAMBADA, ARC-Easy, ARC-C, and HellaSwag. The supplementary material can handle BH-FDR corrections and the Wang et al. differentiation.\n\nAlright, I'm done planning and ready to write. Let me structure this properly: each experiment gets its own section with corresponding table, all numbers pulled directly from the experiment data, clean formatting with booktabs, no em-dashes, and a straightforward academic tone. I'm finalizing the experiment coverage—exp-006 gets mentioned in the limitations section, which accounts for all 11 experiments. Now I'm setting up the notation conventions I'll use throughout (cross-architecture standard deviation, Spearman correlation, Jaccard similarity, and effect magnitude), then I'll start generating the full LaTeX source. I'm also thinking through the table dimensions for the two-column ACL format to make sure everything fits properly. I'm realizing I only have individual accuracy scores for Mamba2 across the different attention ratios, but the cross-architecture standard deviations don't give me the individual scores for GDN, RetNet, or HGRN2. So Table 2 will need to show what I actually have: the Mamba2 accuracies paired with the cross-architecture variability metric.\n\nLet me simplify the table structure to just display the configuration, the standard deviation across three architectures, the standard deviation across four architectures, and the percentage reduction from the baseline. That's clean and tells the story without needing data I don't have.\n\nTime to write the paper now. I'm settling on the formatting details now—numbered sections with `\\section{}` for the ACL style, anonymous authorship, and full-width tables using `\\begin{table*}`. For the bibliography, I'll use the standard natbib format that ACL expects, then write out the complete paper. I'm settling on a clean bibliography format where the short citation shows \"LastName et al.\" for multiple authors and the full year, then I'll structure the paper systematically starting with the document setup, abstract, and main sections covering the introduction, related work, analytical framework, and convergence analysis. I'm planning to keep supplementary details like bootstrap confidence intervals and sensitivity analyses brief or in an appendix to stay within the target word count.\n\nNow I'm writing the LaTeX source... I need to sort out the proper structure for an ACL/EMNLP paper. The Limitations section is mandatory and goes after the conclusion but before references, using an unnumbered section. References don't count toward the page limit, and appendices come after that. So I'm looking at nine numbered sections for the main content within the 8-page limit, then Limitations, References, and optional Appendices.\n\nNow I'm ready to start writing the LaTeX. I'll work through each section carefully, making sure the transitions flow naturally—moving from the introduction into related work by positioning the research within three established areas, and continuing from there with proper argumentation and evidence throughout.\n\nI need to keep in mind that the 7B model experiment was cancelled, so I'll note in Limitations that the analysis covers only the 340M and 1.3B scales due to what's available in Wang et al.'s model zoo. The Wang et al. differentiation experiment should get addressed in the Discussion section rather than as a separate analysis.\n\nWriting the full LaTeX now... a complete, compilable paper.tex file.\n\nFor the acl.sty, I'll copy it to the output directory. Let me include that in the bash command.\n\nOK here's my final plan for tables (revised for conciseness):\n\nTable 1 (table*): 9-Benchmark Convergence Summary\nTable 2 (table): BH-FDR Correction\nTable 3 (table*): Co-evolution (attention ratio × metrics)  \nTable 4 (table): Per-Example Divergence by Benchmark\nTable 5 (table): Sample Characterization\nTable 6 (table): Scale Comparison\n\nThat's 6 tables. Tables 2 and 5 are compact column-width. Tables 1 and 3 are full-width. Tables 4 and 6 are column-width.\n\nThe 4-arch expansion and attention analysis will be described in text with key numbers, while the Wang differentiation goes in Discussion and the cancelled 7B experiment goes in Limitations. But wait—I need a table for every experiment according to the requirements, so let me recount: exp-001 maps to Table 1, exp-002 to Table 4, exp-003 to Table 5, exp-004 to Table 7, and exp-005 to Table 2, which means I need to add another table for the attention analysis.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the table layout to ensure I'm covering all required experiments while keeping the paper to 8 pages.\n\nNow I'm mapping out which experiments go where and ensuring every experiment has corresponding table coverage while staying within the page limit. I've decided to move the Wang differentiation table to the appendix to keep the main text to 8 pages, so now I'm ready to start writing the actual LaTeX code.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":818,"cache_read_input_tokens":44776,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":818},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84f7a4b4-7576-4f2d-a8d0-3b0511753b2b","uuid":"dd3bb645-0e97-473e-b776-f6b568049142"}
